Third-party software in grpc-asr-server
---------------------------------------

grpc-asr-server, the one program this repository builds, links the following native libraries:

- libcurl (curl license), OpenSSL (Apache License 2.0), and FFmpeg shared
  libraries (LGPL-2.1-or-later, described below).
- whisper.cpp, Parakeet and ggml (https://github.com/ggml-org/whisper.cpp, v1.9.3,
  fetched at build time): MIT License.
- miniaudio (third_party/miniaudio/miniaudio.h, vendored): public domain
  (Unlicense) or MIT No Attribution, at your choice.
- gRPC (https://github.com/grpc/grpc, v1.83.0, fetched at build time):
  Apache License 2.0, together with the libraries it builds from its own
  third_party tree (Protocol Buffers, Abseil, BoringSSL, c-ares, RE2, upb,
  zlib), each under the permissive license recorded there.

FFmpeg in the container images (LGPL)
-------------------------------------

The published images (CUDA, CPU and OpenVINO) also contain ffmpeg and
ffprobe under /opt/ffmpeg, with the FFmpeg shared libraries they load
(libavcodec, libavformat, libavfilter, libavutil, libswresample,
libswscale). They are built in each image's build stage from the
upstream FFmpeg 9.0.2 release tarball, checked against a pinned sha256,
by scripts/build-ffmpeg.sh, configured without --enable-gpl,
--enable-version3 or --enable-nonfree with zlib and BSD-licensed OpenH264. OpenH264 supplies the test fixture
encoder and remains under its own license. That makes them licensed under the GNU Lesser General Public
License, version 2.1 or later. The exact configure line for each image is recorded in
/opt/ffmpeg/share/doc/ffmpeg/CONFIGURE; `ffmpeg -buildconf` also prints it.
The CUDA build selects gcc-14. No GPL or nonfree FFmpeg components are enabled.

The same directory holds the license text (COPYING.LGPLv2.1, plus FFmpeg's
LICENSE.md) and the exact source tarball the binaries were built from
(ffmpeg-9.0.2.tar.xz), so every image carries the corresponding source
with it. The build script in this repository is the rest of what is needed
to rebuild and replace the libraries; FFmpeg's own source is also at
https://ffmpeg.org/releases/.

grpc-asr-server dynamically links FFmpeg for the native streaming API.
Its existing collector video/keyframe path also starts ffmpeg and ffprobe
using memfds and pipes. Both paths keep request data off disk.

The build stage also installs Ubuntu's ffmpeg package (an --enable-gpl
build) so the tests can author their media fixtures with its encoders.
It stays in the build stage: no published image contains it.

Every image states the license in its ai.pipestream.ffmpeg.license label
and carries this file at /usr/share/doc/grpc-asr/NOTICE. Like any
Ubuntu-based image, the images also contain the base system's own packages
under their own licenses, recorded under /usr/share/doc.
